# What Expo's build service is sent when the phone app is built (`eas build`,
# run from mobile/). The service is sent the repository, not only the folder the
# command is run in - and when this file is present it is read instead of
# .gitignore, so it has to say everything.
#
# Only the phone app is needed to build the phone app. Nothing else of the
# repository is uploaded: not the server, not the web app, not a document.
#
# NO RULE HERE ENDS IN A SLASH, and that is not a matter of taste. The build
# tool walks the folders and asks about each by its name alone - "mobile", never
# "mobile/" - and a rule that ends in a slash only answers for a name that does.
# Written as `!/mobile/`, the line below never matched, the phone app's folder
# stayed ruled out with everything else, and what would have been sent was
# nothing at all (found on 10 October 2026, before the first upload, by looking:
# `npx eas-cli build:inspect -p android -e lan-test -s archive -o <folder>`).
# mobile/__tests__/buildConfig.test.ts asks the same questions the tool does.
/*
!/mobile

# Of the phone app: what is installed, generated or local to this machine.
/mobile/node_modules
/mobile/.expo
/mobile/dist
/mobile/android
/mobile/ios
/mobile/coverage

# An old copy of a git history that was once inside the phone app's folder.
# It is not the app, it is 106 MB, and what a history holds is not for sending.
/mobile/.git.bak-nested-repo

# Anything local and private that might one day sit beside the app.
/mobile/.env
/mobile/.env.*
/mobile/*.jks
/mobile/*.keystore
/mobile/*.pem
/mobile/*.p12

# mobile/google-services.json is NOT listed: a build needs it, and it is kept
# out of git by not being committed, not by a rule here.
